data: {"structured_response": {"action_intent": "None.", "concise_answer": "The classic ECG findings in hyperkalemia include peaked T waves, prolonged PR interval, widened QRS complex, flattened or absent P wave, and eventually sine wave pattern with ventricular fibrillation. **Important safety warning**: Hyperkalemia can be life-threatening; prompt recognition and treatment are crucial.\n\nHere are some potential ECG changes associated with hyperkalemia, listed by severity of elevation:\n\n* **Mild Hyperkalemia:** Peaked T waves - this is often one of the earliest signs.\n* **Moderate Hyperkalemia:** Prolonged PR interval, Widened QRS complex, Flattening/disappearance of P waves.\n* **Severe Hyperkalemia:** Sine Wave Pattern, Ventricular Fibrillation, Asystole.\n\nIt's important to remember that these are just general guidelines, and individual patients may present differently. Also, other conditions can cause similar ECG abnormalities so it's essential to consider the patient's overall clinical picture when interpreting an ECG.", "display_mode": "concise", "patient_facts_summary": "None.", "local_policy_guidance": "None.", "external_guidance": "None.", "clinical_assessment": "The classic ECG findings in hyperkalemia include peaked T waves, prolonged PR interval, widened QRS complex, flattened or absent P wave, and eventually sine wave pattern with ventricular fibrillation. **Important safety warning**: Hyperkalemia can be life-threatening; prompt recognition and treatment are crucial.", "caveats": "These are general guidelines, and individual patients may present differently. Other conditions can cause similar ECG abnormalities. The severity of ECG changes correlates roughly with potassium levels but is not always reliable.", "escalation": "If you suspect severe hyperkalemia (e.g., sine wave pattern), immediately notify a senior colleague/cardiologist for urgent evaluation and management. Consider checking a serum potassium level if one hasn't been done already.", "cited_sources": []}}

